// case: provided-name-without-lowering
// what: `sort(xs)` named a builtin that `isProvidedName` accepted and no
//      emitter lowered. The front end typed the call, the builder emitted
//      `sort(...)` and the C step was handed a symbol nothing defines.
//      Twenty-six other names had the same shape: the checker passed them and
//      the C step rejected the result.
//
//      A name is provided only when the emitter or the runtime header can
//      resolve it, so the call is an undeclared function and is refused
//      before any C is emitted.
// expect-error: Undeclared function: sort; nothing in scope declares it
fn main() -> int {
    sort([3, 1, 2])
    return 0
}
